  "tldr": "The paper determines the fixed-sample minimax scale at which observational data can detect any nonconstant binary-outcome CATE when the propensity, baseline regression, and CATE have separate rough Hölder indices. The known-propensity experiment reduces by a bounded pseudo-outcome to the classical Spokoiny Hölder goodness-of-fit benchmark, while an explicit profiled fourth-order statistic gives a uniformly calibrated all-regime observational upper envelope and already matches that benchmark in a broad asymmetric region. The unresolved kernel is represented by two independent finite optimizations: \\(r_{\\mathrm{test}}\\) minimizes a fully displayed multiresolution bias and four-projection stochastic envelope, whereas \\(r_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\) maximizes explicit CATE separation subject to a displayed algebraic likelihood-divergence envelope. The common sharp rate is named only if an attaining test and same-class constant-CATE null mixture prove these optimizations equivalent. The final experiment frontier states exactly when revealing the propensity changes detection, without smoothness adaptation, forest certification, or interpreting non-rejection as homogeneity.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Published CATE-homogeneity procedures establish asymptotic validity or power under structured directions and nuisance learner conditions; published minimax results instead treat pointwise CATE estimation, conditional independence under different geometry, signed expected covariance, or heteroskedasticity. None gives the fixed-sample critical radius over the identical binary causal class with three separate Hölder indices and an unknown constant null.",
    "niche": "The missing object is the rough-confounding testing modulus itself, including sparse occupancy, asymmetric propensity and baseline smoothness, boundary logarithms, and the statistical value of revealing the true propensity.",
